    {"topic": "Bronchodilators - Beta-2 Agonists and Anticholinergics", "subtopic": "copd_asthma_treatment", "content": "Beta-2 agonists (albuterol, levalbuterol): rapid bronchodilation via smooth muscle relaxation. Short-acting (SABA, albuterol) onset 5 min, duration 4-6 hours, used as rescue. Long-acting (LABA, salmeterol, formoterol) 12-24 hour duration, maintenance therapy. Anticholinergics (ipratropium, tiotropium): block vagal cholinergic stimulation. LAMA (long-acting muscarinic agonist) maintenance, SAMA (short-acting) rescue. Combination therapy (SABA + SAMA) often used in exacerbations. Side effects: tachycardia, tremor (beta-2), dry mouth (anticholinergic). LABA monotherapy should not be used in asthma (mortality risk).", "tags": ["copd", "asthma", "bronchodilators"], "library": "intern_year_medicine"},
