    {"topic": "Non-invasive Ventilation (BiPAP) - COPD Exacerbation", "subtopic": "copd_treatment", "content": "BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure): non-invasive positive pressure ventilation, reduces need for intubation in COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH <7.35, pCO2 >45). Settings: IPAP 8-12 cmH2O (inspiratory), EPAP 3-5 cmH2O (expiratory). Improves CO2 clearance via increased minute ventilation. Success 50-80% if started early (tiring patient = intubation indication). Contraindications: altered mental status (aspiration risk), respiratory muscle fatigue, active GI bleed, facial/airway trauma. Comfort critical; ramp settings, frequent patient reassurance.", "tags": ["copd", "treatment", "niv"], "library": "intern_year_medicine"},
    {"topic": "Theophylline - Limited Modern Use and Toxicity", "subtopic": "copd_treatment", "content": "Theophylline: methylxanthine bronchodilator, weak beta-2 agonist, phosphodiesterase inhibitor, diuretic. Narrow therapeutic window (10-20 mcg/mL), susceptible to drug interactions (CYP1A2, CYP3A4). Side effects: nausea, vomiting, arrhythmias, seizures (toxicity). Toxicity risk with: hepatic disease, heart failure, concurrent drugs, smoking cessation (levels rise). Limited role; avoided in acute exacerbation (unpredictable absorption/toxicity). Minimal efficacy vs modern bronchodilators. Largely replaced by long-acting bronchodilators. May have role in nocturnal COPD symptoms (long-acting form).", "tags": ["copd", "treatment", "theophylline"], "library": "intern_year_medicine"},
